course: ai
lesson: 24
title: The future: hopes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Nobody can see the future, but people can hope, worry, and make choices that change what happens.
explain: Mia asks whether robots will do all the housework, and Dad says, "I hope so, especially ironing." Real home robots today are still clumsy and expensive: a robot that can fold a crumpled shirt as well as Dad is much harder to build than one that wins at chess, because real homes are messy and every object is different.
explain: Some hopes are big and serious: cures for diseases, help learning in any language. Some worries are big too, and many clever people are working to make powerful AI safe.
explain: The truth about the future is probably somewhere between "wonderful" and "terrible", and it depends on the choices people make, including the ones you will make.
explain: Whatever comes, learning the basics yourself, such as writing, still matters, because it trains your own thinking.
fact: People hope robots and AI will help with boring chores, but real home robots are still clumsy and expensive.
fact: Many scientists hope AI will help find cures for diseases, and some progress has been made, but medicine needs careful testing and takes time.
fact: Some experts worry that very powerful AI could become dangerous, others think that is far off, and many people are working on making AI safe.
fact: Many things people love depend on bodies, feelings and being human, such as watching people play sport.
fact: Many people already use AI as a helper at work, and knowing the basics yourself helps you check it.
fact: Translation and tutoring tools might make learning easier for many people, but not every family can afford them.
fact: The future of AI is probably neither all wonderful nor all terrible; it depends on the choices people make.
fact: Robot pets already exist, but they are not living animals and do not need or feel care the way real pets do.
fact: Self-driving vehicles are being tested, and their safety must be proven before they are used everywhere.
fact: Writing helps you think, not only make words, so learning to write still matters.
exercise: Mia asks if robots will do all the housework one day. What is realistic today? | Home robots are still clumsy and expensive | Every home already has a robot that irons | Robots will do all housework next year | Robots can never do any job at home
exercise: Sam's grandad hopes AI will help find cures. What is the wise way to hope? | Hope with patience, because medicine needs testing | Expect every disease in the world to be cured by next month | Stop going to doctors and wait for AI | Give up hope because AI can't help medicine
exercise: Lily worries AI will become smarter than people and take over. What is the best help? | Talk to a grown-up; many people are working to make AI safe | Stay awake every night watching out for robots coming to take over | Hide all the computers in the house | Believe every scary film about robots
exercise: Connor's friend says "AI will play football." Why does Connor laugh? | People love watching real people play sport | Robots are not allowed on grass | Football is far too easy for an AI to bother with | Connor thinks AI is scared of balls
exercise: Chloe's teacher says you may work alongside AI. What helps most? | Learning the basics well so you can check the AI | Learning nothing at all, because AI will do all of it for you | Learning only how to switch AI on | Waiting until AI teaches you everything
exercise: Maddie dreams of AI helping everyone learn in any language. What should we also think about? | Fairness, so families who can't pay aren't left out | Making it only for rich families | Making everyone in the world speak just one language | Stopping children from learning languages
exercise: Jake hears one grown-up say AI will be wonderful, another terrible. What is probably true? | It is in between and depends on people's choices | The first grown-up is completely right and the second is wrong | The second grown-up is completely right | Nobody can do anything about it
exercise: What is the wise thing Jake can do when grown-ups argue about AI? | Listen to both sides and ask questions | Pick a side at once without thinking | Agree with whoever shouts loudest | Stop thinking about the future at all
exercise: Mia asks, "Will there be AI pets?" What is true? | Robot pets exist, but they are not living animals | AI pets need feeding twice a day | Robot pets will replace all real dogs and cats in a few years | There will never be any robot pets
exercise: Sam imagines self-driving school buses. What must happen first? | Their safety must be proven | Every bus driver must agree first | Children must learn to drive | The buses must learn to sing
exercise: Lily asks if people will still need to learn to write. What is the best answer? | Yes, writing trains your thinking | No, AI will do the writing for everyone | Only people who want to be authors | Only until robots learn to spell well
exercise: Which hope is already showing some progress, according to scientists? | AI helping research into diseases | Robots doing all the ironing at home | Robot pets that truly love their owners | Buses with no drivers in every town
exercise: Why are home robots harder to build than chess programs? | Real homes are messy and every object is different | Chess is much more difficult than ironing, so robots can do housework already | Robots are not allowed to touch clothes | Homes are too small for robots to fit
exercise: What does it mean to be realistic about a hope? | Hope for it but don't expect it tomorrow | Never hope for anything at all, it's pointless | Expect everything you hope for to come at once | Only hope for things that are already here
test: "robots will do ALL my chores by next year, so i'm not tidying my room" Best reply? | Real home robots are still clumsy, so tidy your room | Fair enough, robots are coming next week | Robots will only tidy the rooms of children who wait for them | You never need to tidy if robots exist somewhere
test: What would happen if people stopped learning anything because "AI will do it"? | They could not check whether the AI was right | They would become much cleverer than the AI without even trying | AI would stop making mistakes | Schools would become more important
test: Your friend says, "AI will either save the world or end it, nothing in between." What is fairer? | It's probably in between and depends on people's choices | Your friend is right, there are only two ways it can ever go | Nobody can say anything about it at all | AI has already decided what will happen
test: In a cartoon a robot dog is left without food for a month and still wags its tail. What does it show about robot pets? | They are not living animals and don't need care like real pets | Real dogs can also live without food | Robot dogs secretly eat at night | Cartoons always show real science
test: "can't sleep, scared AI's gonna take over" Kindest and truest reply? | Talk to a grown-up; many clever people are working on keeping AI safe | yes it'll happen tonight, so stay awake and keep watch till morning | Nobody has ever thought about AI safety | Hide your tablet so it can't find you
test: Which hope about AI raises a question of fairness? | Tutoring tools that some families can't afford | Robots doing ironing in every home | Football played only by people | Weather forecasts getting better
test: Sam says self-driving buses should start tomorrow. What is the wise answer? | New technology must be proven safe before it's everywhere | Yes, because self-driving buses could never hurt anyone at all | Yes, but only on rainy days | No, buses can never be made safer
test: Connor's friend says nobody will need footballers soon. What is the best reply? | People love watching people, so many things will stay human | AI will be picked for the national team | All footballers will be replaced by robot players by next year | Football will be banned for robots only
test: Which reason for learning to write is the strongest? | Writing helps you think clearly | Writing is needed to switch on AI | Writing is only for old-fashioned letters | Writing makes your hand stronger
test: Grandad expects AI to cure his illness next week. What is honest and kind? | AI may help medicine, but it is slow and needs testing | Yes, all the cures will arrive next week, so there's no need to worry | No, AI will never help any patient | Stop taking his medicine and wait
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present capability of domestic robots? | They remain comparatively clumsy and expensive | They perform all household tasks to a high standard | They are installed in the majority of households | They are prohibited by law from domestic use
exam: It is given that two adults disagree as to whether artificial intelligence will prove beneficial or harmful. Identify the conclusion most consistent with the lesson. | The outcome is likely to lie between the extremes and to depend on human choices | The outcome has been determined already by the machines themselves and cannot be altered | The first opinion must be accepted without question | Neither opinion merits any consideration
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning robotic pets. | They exist but are not living animals requiring care | They are living animals constructed in laboratories | They require feeding and veterinary attention | They have not yet been manufactured anywhere
exam: In respect of the instruction of children in writing, which of the following statements is most accurate? | Writing remains valuable as an exercise of thought | Writing has been rendered wholly obsolete by machines | Writing is required solely of prospective authors | Writing is to be taught only until machines spell correctly
